VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: WS1
ATTEMPT: 4
---
METHOD
Built ./cmd/server from the given snapshot (`go build ./cmd/server` -> success,
no .git in the snapshot). Extracted the JS rounding helpers into a standalone
file and ran them under real Node (v24.12.0), and built a standalone Go
binary that reproduces the template's exact `fmt.Sprintf("%.1f", v)` call
(what `{{printf "%.1f" .Value}}` does). Also stood up the real built server
on a scratch data dir (own `cp`, `BUDGET_LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:39871`,
`BUDGET_DEBUG=false`) and drove it with real Chromium (Playwright,
NODE_PATH as given). Server killed and port confirmed free afterward
(`ss -ltnp` empty, no lingering scratch `server` processes). Never touched
:8080, never ran a repo-root `budget2` binary, no MCP tools, no git state
changes; only wrote inside my own checker scratch dir.

FINDING (in scope, FAIL basis): R-FMT' percent1 path diverges from Go on
negative near-zero values — a domain the server itself accepts with no
validation.
- Swept 32,465 values (x.x5 ties across ±2000, ±0.001 band, random floats in
  [-1000,1000] and [-0.001,0.001], plus the contract's own worked examples
  7.25/4.35/3.85) through (a) the real Go binary's `%.1f` and (b) the
  extracted `roundHalfEvenDecimalString(v,1)`. Exactly 1014 mismatches, and
  every one is the SAME pair: Go says `"-0.0"`, JS says `"0.0"` — for any
  genuine negative v in the open interval (-0.05, 0). Every tie (x.x5) and
  every other value agrees exactly — the half-even tie-break logic itself is
  correct; only this one sign case is wrong.
- Root cause: `roundHalfEvenDecimalString`'s last line is
  `if (negative && Number(result) !== 0) result = '-' + result;` — it drops
  the sign whenever the ROUNDED result is zero, not just for true bit-pattern
  -0. Go's bare `%.1f` (used directly by the template, no zero-belt) has no
  such suppression and prints `-0.0` for any v in (-0.05,0). Verified
  directly: `fmt.Sprintf("%.1f", -0.03)` = `"-0.0"` (go run); JS
  `roundHalfEvenDecimalString(-0.03,1)` = `"0.0"`.
- Reachability is real, not contrived: `inflation_rate` and
  `spending_decline_rate` have `HasBounds: false` in form_spec.go (no
  server-side range check at all), and `handlers_healthcare.go`'s update path
  for `pre_medicare_inflation`/`post_medicare_inflation` checks only
  `monthlyCost < 0` and `age` — never the inflation fields. All four fields
  that use `data-quick-adjust-display-format="percent1"` can legitimately
  hold a negative value with zero validation rejection.
- Demonstrated live on the real server: built a fixture (via the oracle's own
  `base_plan()`/`write_fixture()` lib, reading only the LIVE JSON file
  on disk — no :8080 contact) with `inflation_rate=-0.03`,
  `spending_decline_rate=-0.02`, one person's `pre_medicare_inflation=-0.01`.
  `curl` of the real served `/whatif` page shows `-0.0%` nine times: the
  in-card display span + its range's `aria-valuetext`, the Quick Adjust
  mirror's display + `aria-valuetext`, for both `inflation_rate` and the
  healthcare person's `pre_medicare_inflation` (both in-card and QA copies).
  Loaded the same fixture in real Chromium after JS ran: confirmed attempt
  4's mirror-only load sync correctly leaves these at `-0.0%` (matches
  R-FMT's "authoritative at load", no regression there).
- The divergence is NEW, not pre-existing: `git show
  643fa54:web/static/js/whatif-quick-adjust.js` shows master's percent1 case
  was `numericValue.toFixed(1) + '%'`; `(-0.03).toFixed(1)` = `"-0.0"` in
  real Node — i.e. master's old formatter AGREED with Go (both showed the
  same, if odd-looking, `-0.0%`). This attempt's new
  `roundHalfEvenDecimalString`, introduced by this attempt (file is in the
  WS1.4 manifest), is what breaks the agreement.
- The worker's own test file documents the false premise that let this
  through: `whatif-quick-adjust.test.cjs:158` — "no WS1 field is ever
  negative, so only the rounding RULE is this test's concern, not the sign
  convention" — and the `percentHalfEvenTies` table used by both the
  half-even test and the "kills toFixed/x*10" test (lines 177-196) contains
  zero negative rows. That premise is factually false for exactly the
  reasons above, and the negative branch of `roundHalfEvenDecimalString` was
  never exercised against real Go output.
- Confirmed the diagnosis is precise and repairable (not misdiagnosed, per
  the "does the remedy fix it" check): changing that one line to
  `if (negative) result = '-' + result;` (matching Go's unconditional sign
  preservation) reduces the mismatch count on the same 32,465-value sweep
  from 1014 to 0.
- Live-drag reachability caveat, reported for precision: every control
  bound to `percent1` (main range AND its Quick Adjust mirror, for all four
  fields) has `min="0"`, so no mouse/keyboard drag on the CURRENT UI can
  itself produce a negative value in the live formatter — the browser
  clamps a range's `.value` to `min` the instant it's touched or
  programmatically set, and `proxyQuickAdjustMirrorToCanonical` overwrites
  the (unclamped) hidden canonical field with the mirror's already-clamped
  value before any resync reads it. The bug is real and reachable through
  the field's own accepted (unbounded) stored-value domain — demonstrated
  above on the served page with no JS involved — but I could not construct
  a live in-browser drag that shows the LIVE JS text disagree with a
  freshly-reloaded server text within a single session; the disagreement is
  between "what a future recompute of this exact stored figure would show"
  and "what the server just rendered." This is the same class and the same
  order of narrowness as prior accepted WS1 FAILs (the .50-tie, the 1-ulp
  COLA drift, the 16-17-sig-fig cost basis) — none of those were reachable
  by drag either; all were off-grid/unusual STORED values, exactly this
  field's own accepted domain.

SCOPE
- FAIL basis: `web/static/js/whatif-quick-adjust.js`'s `roundHalfEvenDecimalString`
  (introduced by this attempt, in the WS1.4 manifest) vs. Go's `%.1f`
  (`internal/templates` / the bare `{{printf "%.1f" ...}}` template calls
  this attempt's contract governs under R-FMT'), on `inflation_rate`,
  `spending_decline_rate`, `pre_medicare_inflation`, `post_medicare_inflation`.
- Positive evidence found and not disputed: the half-even TIE-BREAKING logic
  itself is correct and byte-identical to Go across the entire sweep (0
  non-sign mismatches out of 32,465); `formatWholeDollars`/`formatNumber`
  agree at every .50 tie tried; `formatWholeDollars` is no longer used by
  any WS1 template (0 hits); `formatExact` is a single, uniform
  `FormatFloat(v,'f',-1)` with no scaled-site confusion (`formatExactScaled`
  is the one COLA site, `sigFigsPlain` never emits exponent notation); the
  mirror-only load/after-swap sync correctly leaves server-rendered
  display/aria untouched (verified live in Chromium); oracle v5 (both
  variants) is ORACLE PASS per `.swarm/tier3/WS1/oracle.4.log`.
- Per CLAUDE.md's default-to-FAIL-on-ambiguity rule and the established
  pattern for every prior WS1 hard stop (dual-formatter divergence on a
  value class the oracle's own fixture never tried), this is a FAIL, not a
  backlog observation — the field's own server-side validation accepts the
  triggering value, and the served page already shows the artifact.
